      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{150}^{∘}).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{150}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$$\n-{150}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={210}^{∘}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{150}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{210}^{∘}.$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254214/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${210}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{210}^{∘}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{150}^{∘})=-\\frac{1}{2}$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
