    "q-64924": {
      "dom_id": "step-q64924",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254214/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})}=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=-\\sqrt{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
