    "q-123658": {
      "dom_id": "step-q123658",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-{300}^{∘})=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{300}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$$\n-{300}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={60}^{∘}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-{300}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sec}⁡({60}^{∘}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254214/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${60}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡({60}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-{300}^{∘})=2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
