    "q-64942": {
      "dom_id": "step-q64942",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Determine the value of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-{210}^{∘}).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{210}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$$\n-{210}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={150}^{∘}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-{210}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cot}⁡({150}^{∘}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254214/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${150}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{150}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡({150}^{∘})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({150}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({150}^{∘})}=\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
